CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 11 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ sz<char> == 1: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ sz<int> == sizeof\(int\) \(distinct entity\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG: sz<int>==1 only under collision \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[temp.spec]/4 + [temp.inst]/2: each specialization of a member function
template is a distinct entity.  When the template parameters are not reflected
in the parameter types (e.g. `template<class U> static constexpr unsigned
sz()`), the specializations share a function signature; CBMC used to name them
all with the same unsuffixed member symbol, so sz<char> and sz<int> collided on
one symbol and shared a body (sz<int> wrongly returned 1).  A constexpr member
function template specialization now gets a distinct symbol that encodes its
template arguments.  Assertions 1-2 prove the specializations are independent
with correct values; assertion 3 (which held only under the unsound collision)
must now FAIL, proving non-vacuity.
